    'primer-dimer-checker' => [
        'title' => 'Primer Dimer Checker: Self-Pairs, Hairpins and Risk',
        'desc' => 'Check a primer pair for complementary runs, 3-prime self-complementarity, hairpins and cross-dimers, and read a single risk rating for the pair.',
        'intro' => 'Primer dimers consume primers before they can amplify anything, and they show up as a low-molecular-weight band rather than an error. This page checks a pair for the complements that make dimers form and summarises the result as a risk rating.',
        'does' => 'It looks for the patterns that let primers pair with themselves or with each other: a complementary run anywhere in the pair, a run at the 3′ end where extension can start, and a hairpin where a primer folds back on itself.',
        'how' => 'The page takes each primer and builds its reverse complement, then slides the two against each other to find the longest complementary stretch. A run anywhere in the pair is reported as potential dimerisation; a run at the 3′ end is reported separately because that is the end a polymerase extends from, so a 3′ dimer is far worse than an internal one. The same alignment run against the primer itself gives the hairpin length, and the risk rating combines the length of the worst 3′ run with the hairpin figures.',
        'example_note' => 'A 19-mer and a 20-mer with no 3′ complementarity still show an eight-base run internally, and a five-base hairpin in each. The risk of four reflects the internal run rather than a 3′ problem — the pair will probably work, but it is not a clean pair.',
        'read' => 'Read the 3′ figures first: a complementary run of three or more bases at the 3′ end is the one that ruins reactions. Internal runs of six to eight bases are common and usually tolerable. Hairpins under about five bases rarely matter; longer ones can hide the 3′ end from the polymerase.',
        'defs' => [
            '3′ complementary run' => 'the longest complementary stretch at the 3′ end, where extension begins.',
            'Longest complementary run' => 'the longest complement anywhere between the two primers.',
            'Self-pair, forward' => 'the longest stretch where the forward primer is complementary to itself.',
            'Hairpin, forward' => 'the longest fold-back a single primer can form with itself.',
            'Risk' => 'a combined rating from the 3′ and internal figures.',
        ],
        'check' => 'Write the reverse complement of the forward primer and line it up against the reverse primer by hand. The longest run where the letters pair is the figure the page reports. Then look at whether that run reaches the 3′ end.',
        'pitfalls' => [
            'Reading the wrong end' => 'A 3′ run is the dangerous one. The same run moved five bases inward is usually harmless.',
            'Sequence direction' => 'The reverse primer must be entered 5′ to 3′ as ordered, not as it appears in the template.',
            'Ignoring concentration' => 'Dimer formation depends on concentration and temperature as well as sequence; a borderline pair can be fine at low concentration.',
        ],
        'limits' => [
            'Two primers only' => 'Multiplex reactions need every pair checked, which this page does one pair at a time.',
            'No thermodynamics' => 'The runs are counted, not scored by nearest-neighbour thermodynamics, so the rating is a guide rather than a free energy.',
            'No product level' => 'It checks the primers against each other, not against the template.',
        ],
        'uses' => 'Dimer checks are run before ordering a pair, when a reaction produces a band at the bottom of the gel instead of a product, and when a primer that worked at one concentration stops working at another.',
        'extra' => 'When a pair rates badly, the cheapest fix is usually to move the 3′ end of the worse primer by a base or two rather than to redesign both. Re-running the check after that change is quick, and it is the change that most often turns a failing pair into a working one without touching the rest of the protocol.',
        'faq' => [
            ['q' => 'What counts as a bad 3′ run?', 'a' => 'Three or more complementary bases at the 3′ end. That is enough for a polymerase to extend the dimer and consume both primers.'],
