    'protein-isoelectric-point' => [
        'title' => 'Protein Isoelectric Point Calculator with Net Charge',
        'desc' => 'Paste a protein to find its isoelectric point and net charge at a chosen pH, with length, mass, acidic and basic residue counts and GRAVY alongside.',
        'intro' => 'The isoelectric point is the pH at which a protein carries no net charge. This page finds it by titration: it computes the charge at a chosen pH from the sequence, then searches for the pH where that charge crosses zero.',
        'does' => 'It reports the pH at which the protein is neutral, the charge it carries at the pH you specify, and the composition figures that explain both: length, mass, acidic and basic residue counts, and GRAVY.',
        'how' => 'Every ionisable group in the sequence — the carboxylates of aspartate and glutamate, the amines of lysine and arginine, histidine\'s imidazole, the termini, and cysteine and tyrosine where present — is given a pKa. At a given pH each group\'s charge follows from the Henderson-Hasselbalch relation, and the charges are summed. The page evaluates the sum across the pH range and finds the point where it crosses zero: that is the isoelectric point. The charge at your chosen pH is the same sum evaluated once.',
        'example_note' => 'A 29-residue peptide with two acidic and six basic residues gives a pI of 10.41 and a charge of +3.03 at pH 7 — strongly basic, as the residue counts predict. GRAVY of −0.476 says it is hydrophilic anyway, which is the combination that keeps small basic peptides soluble.',
        'read' => 'The pI is a single number with a large practical effect: below it the protein is positive and binds cation exchangers, above it the protein is negative and binds anion exchangers. A pI far from 7 also means the protein carries a strong net charge at physiological pH, which affects how it runs on a gel and how it behaves in a buffer.',
        'defs' => [
            'Isoelectric point' => 'the pH where the summed charge is zero.',
            'Net charge' => 'the sum of all group charges at the pH you entered.',
            'Acidic residues' => 'aspartate and glutamate — negative at neutral pH.',
            'Basic residues' => 'lysine, arginine and histidine — positive at neutral pH.',
            'GRAVY' => 'average hydropathy; a positive value means the chain is hydrophobic overall.',
        ],
        'check' => 'Count the acidic and basic residues: a protein with many more basic than acidic residues must have a pI above 7, and the further the counts diverge, the further from 7 it goes. For the charge at a chosen pH, remember that a residue counts as fully charged only when the pH is two units away from its pKa.',
        'pitfalls' => [
            'Terminal charges' => 'The N- and C-termini are charged too. On a short peptide they matter as much as any residue.',
            'Cysteines and tyrosines' => 'Their pKa values are high and their contribution depends on whether they are protonated, which the sequence does not say.',
            'Modifications' => 'Phosphorylation adds negative charge that no residue count will show.',
        ],
        'limits' => [
            'Model pKa values' => 'The pKa of a group depends on its local environment in a folded protein; a sequence-based calculation cannot see that.',
            'No structure' => 'Buried groups titrate differently from exposed ones, and this page treats them alike.',
            'Standard residues only' => 'Unusual residues and modified termini are not modelled.',
        ],
        'uses' => 'The pI is used to choose a buffer for ion-exchange chromatography, to choose the pH of a gel, to predict whether a protein will precipitate at low salt, and to explain why a protein sticks to one column and not another.',
        'extra' => 'The practical use of a calculated pI is not the number itself but the margin you leave around it. Running an ion-exchange column at a pH within half a unit of the pI gives a protein that is neither reliably positive nor reliably negative, so it binds badly and elutes in a broad peak. Two units away, the behaviour is predictable.',
        'faq' => [
            ['q' => 'Why does my calculated pI differ from the one in a database?', 'a' => 'Because databases use different pKa sets and sometimes different terminal handling. A difference of a few tenths is normal.'],
            ['q' => 'What pH should I run an ion-exchange column at?', 'a' => 'At least one unit away from the pI, on the side that gives the protein the charge the column wants.'],
        ],
    ],

